  "query_preview": "..Water vapor shows a shortwave moving into northern\nArizona from Nevada. Models keep this low on a southerly track,\nmoving through Arizona today and into Sonora by Saturday\nmorning. As the low travels south today it will bring up modest\nmoisture increasing PWAT values to 0.7\"-0.8\" from Tucson\neastward, which is about 150-200 percent of normal for this time\nof year. This same area will see broad synoptic scale ascent as\nwell as instability of 200 J/kg-800 J/kg. This will lead to\nscattered showers and thunderstorms this afternoon and evening\nfor the eastern half of the CWA. Tucson looks like it will be\non western periphery of convective activity with isolated storms\npossible in town. This is supported by the REFS along with NBM \nPoPs. The best chances look to be in Cochise County and southern\nGraham and Greenlee Counties. Some of these areas could see \nrain totals of 0.5\"+ through Saturday morning with very \nlocalized higher amounts in the higher terrain like Dos Cabezas.\nBecause of this there is a localized flash flood threat with \nWPC putting Cochise County and southern Graham/Greenlee Counties\nunder a marginal risk for heavy rainfall. Outside of these \nareas expect rain totals generally between 0.01\"-0.30\". There \nisn't much of a wind threat, but stronger convective cells could\nproduce wind gusts to 40 mph. \n\nIsolated activity will be possible overnight into Saturday\nmorning as the low moves into Sonora. By Saturday afternoon any\nremaining showers or thunderstorms will be confined to Cochise\nCounty and fairly weak. At the same time an easterly pressure\ngradient sets up over the region leading to breezy easterly\nwinds Saturday morning into Sunday. Expect wind speeds\ngenerally 12-20 mph with gusts to 30 mph. Winds will be\nstrongest Saturday morning and early afternoon and again Sunday\nmorning into the early afternoon. These easterly winds will also\nlikely keep low temperatures warmer than they otherwise would be \nSaturday and Sunday mornings.\n\nAloft, by Monday, the shortwave will be in Texas with ridging\nmoving into the Southwest through at least the middle of next\nweek. High temperatures will be a few degrees below normal \nthrough Sunday, then warm to around normal as the ridge moves in\nnext week. Then late next week there is potential for another\ntrough to move into the region.",
